{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/session-task-7db5e3d5-review.md", "content": "# Review: heterogeneous demonstrations without a validated common rule

This instance supplied three sharply different pairs: a 21×21 sparse diagonal-color scene became a full 5-color periodic field; a 23×23 otherwise blank grid with two bottom stripes became an alternating two-color row pattern; and a 2×2 near-uniform patch had its lone zero filled. The 12×12 test instead contained offset diagonal tracks in colors 8, 2, 6, and 7. The demonstrations were evidence of radically different surface transformations, but no cumulative exact-delta analysis produced a validated shared selector or output mapping. Serial demo requests (three were used) did not resolve the relationship; the last action continued requesting rather than making an evidence-based decision.

Reusable lesson: task IDs and stream membership alone do not justify assuming a common pixel-level operation. Record each pair's exact delta and output structure before requesting another. Explicitly test whether the task may be an abstract transformation shared across different visual classes; formulate at least two candidate abstractions and derive predictions that can be replayed against all examples. If no candidate maps all demonstrations exactly, another request is justified only if its predicted outcomes discriminate those candidates. Do not invent a test output by blending the demo patterns or select one demo's operation based on superficial resemblance. Keep the action protocol strict: one JSON action only on the final line.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen/play/users/u047/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/session-task-7db5e3d5-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/compact-region-mapping.md", "content": "# Compact region-to-output mapping

When a partitioned input has a much smaller output, investigate a region summary as a live task class instead of assuming a same-size edit or stamp. Identify separator rows/columns, compute the number and arrangement of regions, and associate each region with the corresponding output cell. Inspect marker presence, marker color/identity, count, and position within each region as candidate encodings. Verify the exact output—including default values for empty regions—against every demonstration before transfer. Output dimensions alone do not establish the mapping. Before spending another demo request, list at least two candidate encodings and state what observation in the next pair would distinguish them.

In a demo with sparse markers embedded in a regular partitioned canvas, the compact output preserved a 6×3 region arrangement and represented localized marker colors at corresponding output positions; most empty positions retained the canvas color. Treat this as a diagnostic pattern, not a universal rule: confirm separators and correspondence in the current task.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen/play/users/u047/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-delta-discipline/references/compact-region-mapping.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/session-task-36d8805c-review.md", "content": "# Session review: heterogeneous demonstrations and unsupported projections

In this instance the demos ranged from small to very wide grids and used disparate palettes and structures. Observed effects included narrow line fills around isolated markers, multi-directional fills/rays, and replacement of marker cells with 4; one demo also altered a uniform-color region into a rectangular extension. The test had sparse color-3 cells plus color-2 triplets on opposite borders. I inferred a vertical extension from one marker and converted all markers to 4, but that candidate was rejected. The next attempt assumed horizontal fills from the edge runs and was also rejected. These outputs were not validated by exact replay against all demos, and the examples were too heterogeneous to justify transferring that simple rule confidently.

Lesson: after an incorrect submission, do not select one visually appealing delta from the latest demo and apply it to the test. Compare the entire demo set, check dimensions/palette/task-class coherence, and explicitly derive a rule that predicts all changed and unchanged cells. For request decisions, state competing hypotheses and the exact observation needed to distinguish them; additional examples without a specific discriminating purpose are costly but may still fail to make a drifting/non-identifiable mapping coherent. Do not present a speculative grid as evidence-based.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen/play/users/u047/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/session-task-36d8805c-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-stream-demo-discipline", "file": "references/grid-serialization-validation.md", "content": "# Grid serialization check

A visually copied or manually assembled output can be invalid even when the task rule is understood. Before submission, verify mechanically that the output has exactly the expected number of rows and that each row has exactly the expected number of cells. Check values are integers in the allowed range, then serialize the same validated grid. Do not infer successful dimensionality from the visual layout: inspect all row lengths, since one omitted background cell in a row is enough to make a nominal 19x17 output malformed.
